==In spin-off games==
[[File:Saffron City SSB.png|thumb|200px|Silph Co. in [[Super Smash Bros.]]]]
===Super Smash Bros. series===
{{main|Saffron City (stage)}}
Silph Co. appears in [[Saffron City]] as a playable stage in ''[[Super Smash Bros.]]'' (called '''Silf''' in the Japanese version). The {{pkmn|battle}} takes place on the roof of Silph Co. and a few smaller surrounding buildings, along with two small moving platforms. This is the home stage of {{p|Pikachu}} and {{p|Jigglypuff}} in the game.

Certain Pokémon will emerge from the elevator on the Silph Co. building and attack briefly, similar to Pokémon summoned from {{DL|Poké Ball (item)|Super Smash Bros. series|Poké Balls}}. Occasionally, {{p|Pidgey}}, {{p|Fearow}}, {{p|Butterfree}}, and other {{type|Flying}} Pokémon will appear in the background.

The stage returns in ''[[Super Smash Bros. Ultimate]]'' (in which the Japanese language setting uses the official romanization '''Sylph'''), with its visuals mostly cleaned up with higher-resolution textures; however, the Pokémon that appear maintain their pixellated, two-dimensional appearance from the original game.

===Pokémon Quest===
In [[Pokémon Quest]], the [[MoBee|MoBee IV]] drone that accompanies the player is mentioned to have been manufactured by Silph Co. When the price of the MoBee IV was reduced, Silph Co. also reduced the capacity of its battery. Silph Co. does not appear in the game other than being named in reference to MoBee IV. 

===Pokémon Pokopia===
In [[Pokémon Pokopia]], the ruined remains of Silph Co. can be found on the central island of the [[Sparkling Skylands]]. When {{poko|Ditto}} meets [[Tinkmaster]] the {{p|Tinkaton}}, she will task them with restoring the building in the hopes of attracting humans to return. The renovation project comprises of four separate phases, each requiring different materials and Pokémon to assist with the construction. Once the project is fully completed, {{p|Mewtwo}} appears on the roof and briefly meets with Ditto before departing. 

Unlike past depictions of the Silph Co. headquarters, this version consists of only four stories and a rooftop area. An underground sewer system runs around the perimeter of the building, and a ruined subway tunnel is situated deep below the building's foundations. Initially, the building is in an extreme state of disrepair, with most floors having deteriorated from exposure to the elements. The elevator is non-functional, though a stairwell is present behind the building to provide access to each level on foot. Each phase of the restoration will repair the elevator for its respective floor. The player is unable to make any modifications to the building themselves until restoration work is completed. 

* '''1F:''' Lobby, with the layout resembling that seen in the mainline games. This floor is mostly intact, other than some broken window panes and missing sections of the walls.
* '''2F:''' Appears to have been an office working area. The first renovation project converts into a workshop, with a large play area for Pokémon to relax in.
* '''3F:''' Appears to have been a research lab. The second renovation project converts into a canteen for humans and Pokémon to enjoy meals together in.
* '''4F:''' The top floor contains the president's office. The third renovation project largely restores its original opulent design, but refocuses it as a conference space rather than a personal office. A small executive bathroom is also added.
* '''Rooftop:''' Initially, almost the entire roof of the building is missing. The third renovation project restores it, and converts the space into a terrace garden with a shop.

Although the renovation adds many items that require power, there are no utility poles or transmitters in the building whatsoever, nor any power generators on the island. The player must add their own to make these items functional. Additionally, the cement mixer in the workshop lacks a water source, so a nearby block must be removed and filled with water to make it operational. Despite the restoration work done to the building, no repairs are made at all to the stairs behind the building, nor the lobby interior.
{{-}}

==In animation==
[[File:Game Freak Hitmonlee poster.png|thumb|210px|Poster from the [[Game Freak]] website]]
===''Pokémon the Series''===
According to artwork from the [[Game Freak]] website, the [[P1 Grand Prix]] is sponsored by Silph Co. and held in the [[Celadon City]] Hall.

===Pokémon Origins===
[[File:Silph Company PO.png|thumb|250px|left|Silph Co. in [[Pokémon Origins]]]]
Silph Co. appeared in ''[[PO03|File 3: Giovanni]]''. Like in the {{pkmn|games}}, it was taken over by [[Team Rocket]]. Hearing from Silph Co.'s vice-president of how Team Rocket was experimenting on Pokémon in order to accelerate the completion of [[Master Ball]], {{OBP|Red|Origins}} decided to storm the building. Doing so, he freed all the Pokémon and {{tc|Scientist}}s that were trapped inside, even obtaining a {{p|Lapras}} from one of the Scientists as a thank-you gift. Finally he arrived at the president's office, where he encountered [[Giovanni]]. Before Giovanni could escape, Red proclaimed that he would always be out there to stop their plans, irritating the Team Rocket Boss. Wanting to teach Red a lesson, Giovanni challenged Red to a Pokémon battle, which ended with Giovanni's {{p|Nidoqueen}} defeating [[Red's Charizard]] and wrecking a big portion of the building in the process. Afterwards, Giovanni escaped with a helicopter, leaving before the police could arrive. It was also mentioned that the development of Master Ball was put on hold for a while due to this incident.

===Pokémon Generations===
A photograph of Silph Co. appeared in ''[[PG02|The Chase]]'', where an [[International Police]] agent informed [[Looker]] about Team Rocket's failed attempt to take over the building while giving him a report about their attempt to locate Giovanni.
{{-}}

==In the manga==
===Pokémon Adventures===
[[File:Silph Co Adventures.png|200px|thumb|Silph Co. in [[Pokémon Adventures]]]]
Instead of an office building, the Silph Co. in Pokémon Adventures appears to be more like a typical villain base, having multiple traps and specifically designed rooms set up in case of intruders.

===={{MangaArc|Red, Green & Blue}}====
Silph Co. first appeared in ''[[PS026|Holy Moltres]]'', where [[Team Rocket]] was shown keeping their base inside the building and having managed to capture the three {{adv|legendary birds}}. Later it was revealed that Team Rocket was keeping the kidnapped people of [[Pallet Town]] at the building, prompting {{adv|Red}} and {{adv|Blue}} to break into the Team Rocket-infested Saffron City and storm the Silph Co. building. {{adv|Green}} also entered the building, although with much more selfish motives.

Inside the Silph Co. building, Red, Blue, and Green were confronted by Team Rocket's commanders: the evil [[Gym Leader]]s [[Lt. Surge]], [[Koga]], and [[Sabrina]]. During the conflict, the legendary birds were accidentally {{pkmn|fusion|fused}} together by Green's use of the "Badge Energy Amplifier", a device that increases the energy produced by [[Badge|Gym Badges]]. With their combined efforts, the three Pallet Town Trainers managed to defeat the fusion Pokémon and de-fuse it, allowing the legendary birds to escape. The building collapsed in the ensuing chaos, bringing Team Rocket's plans to an end.

===={{MangaArc|Yellow}}====
Two years later in ''[[PS041|Ponyta Tale]]'', [[Agatha]] was seen picking up the Badge Energy Amplifier from the Silph Co. ruins for usage at [[Cerise Island]].

